{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_wcbmgot2yk36zeqrrdc3a2i2ri3t3ejtumghj2lzyxghznqdw4xq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01T9bx7NUuuRvWuXCabxD8T2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > SYMBOLS.md <<'HEADER'\n# SYMBOLS.md — exported-symbol parity (Phase A / Phase D)\n\nDerived mechanically from `nm -D --defined-only` on both shared libraries.\n\n* **C reference `.so`** — `cc -shared -fPIC c_src/src/main.c c_src/src/inventory.c\n  -Ic_src/include -o libdriver_c.so` (`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` builds those same two\n  translation units; it just links them as an executable, so the shared-library\n  form is built with the identical sources/flags plus `-shared -fPIC`).\n  Built automatically by `build.rs` into `$OUT_DIR/libdriver_c.so`.\n* **Rust `.so`** — `target/<profile>/libdriver.so`, from `crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"rlib\"]`.\n\nReproduce with:\n\n```sh\n./run_all.sh            # builds both and diffs the symbol sets\n# or manually:\nnm -D --defined-only $(ls target/debug/build/driver-*/out/libdriver_c.so | head -1) | awk '{print $3}' | sort > /tmp/c.txt\nnm -D --defined-only target/debug/libdriver.so                                     | awk '{print $3}' | sort > /tmp/r.txt\ncomm -23 /tmp/c.txt /tmp/r.txt   # symbols missing from Rust  -> must be empty\ncomm -13 /tmp/c.txt /tmp/r.txt   # symbols only in Rust       -> must be empty\n```\n\n## Result\n\n| metric | value |\n|--------|-------|\n| symbols defined by C `.so` | **63** |\n| symbols defined by Rust `.so` | **63** |\n| C symbols missing from Rust `.so` | **0** |\n| extra non-C symbols exported by Rust `.so` | **0** |\n| undefined non-libc symbols in Rust `.so` | **0** (only `libc`/`libgcc`/`ld` imports; see below) |\n\nUndefined symbols in the C `.so` are all libc: `__isoc99_sscanf`, `fgets`, `free`,\n`malloc`, `printf`, `putchar`, `puts`, `realloc`, `stdin`, `strcmp`, `strncpy`\n(plus the weak `__gmon_start__`, `__cxa_finalize`, `_ITM_*` stubs every ELF gets).\nThe Rust `.so` imports the same allocator/IO primitives (`malloc`, `realloc`,\n`free`, `write`, `read`, …) from libc, plus the usual `pthread_*`/`dl*`\ndependencies of Rust's `std`; nothing from the C translation unit is left\nundefined.\n\n## Every C symbol, its origin, and whether the Rust `.so` exports it\n\nAll 63 are `T` (global text) in both libraries. The `array_*` / `list_*` names are\ngenerated by the `DEFINE_ARRAY(TYPE)` / `DEFINE_LIST(TYPE)` macros in\n`generic_containers.h`, instantiated for `int`, `double`, `item_t` and `order_t`\nby `inventory.c`; the Rust side generates them with the `define_array!` /\n`define_list!` macros in `src/capi.rs`, so the macro-generated names exist there\ntoo.\n\n| symbol | C origin | exported by Rust `.so` |\n|--------|----------|------------------------|\nHEADER\ncat target/symrows.md >> SYMBOLS.md\ncat >> SYMBOLS.md <<'FOOTER'\n\n## Notes on individual symbols\n\n* `main` — `main.c` is part of the shared library, so the C `.so` exports `main`.\n  The Rust `cdylib` exports it from `src/capi.rs` (`#[no_mangle] pub unsafe\n  extern \"C\" fn main() -> c_int`, delegating to `demos::c_main`). It is excluded\n  only under `cfg(test)`, where the unit-test harness provides its own `main`;\n  the shipped `cdylib` always has it (verified by `nm`). The `driver` executable\n  does **not** link the library crate — it compiles the same modules directly and\n  supplies a normal Rust `fn main` — so there is no duplicate-`main` link error.\n* `find_expensive_items` — declared in `inventory.h` and defined in\n  `inventory.c`, but never called from `main.c`. It is exported by both `.so`s and\n  is covered by the differential tests (`tests/inventory_funcs.rs`,\n  `tests/errors.rs`) even though no demo exercises it.\n* `array_*_get` — no bounds or NULL check in C; the Rust translation performs the\n  same unchecked raw read so that in-bounds behaviour (including reading elements\n  that are still present after `clear`) matches byte for byte.\n* Struct-by-value ABI: `print_item(item_t)`, `print_order(order_t)`,\n  `create_item(...) -> item_t`, `create_order(...) -> order_t` pass/return\n  `item_t` (120 bytes) and `order_t` (80 bytes) by value. The Rust `#[repr(C)]`\n  definitions reproduce the C layout exactly, verified against the C compiler:\n\n  | type | size | align | field offsets |\n  |------|------|-------|---------------|\n  | `item_t` | 120 | 8 | `id` 0, `name` 4, `category` 68, `price` 104, `quantity` 112 |\n  | `order_t` | 80 | 8 | `customer_id` 0, `customer_name` 4, `total_amount` 72 |\n  | `array_TYPE_t` | 24 | 8 | `data` 0, `size` 8, `capacity` 16 |\n  | `list_TYPE_t` | 24 | 8 | `head` 0, `tail` 8, `size` 16 |\n  | `list_node_int_t` | 16 | 8 | `data` 0, `next` 8 |\n  | `list_node_item_t_t` | 128 | 8 | `data` 0, `next` 120 |\n  | `list_node_order_t_t` | 88 | 8 | `data` 0, `next` 80 |\n\n  Because the Rust containers also allocate through the same libc\n  `malloc`/`realloc`/`free`, a container created by one library is byte-identical\n  to one created by the other; the tests exploit this by handing C-built\n  containers to Rust entry points and vice versa.\nFOOTER\nwc -l SYMBOLS.md","description":"Write SYMBOLS.md"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1948,"cache_read_input_tokens":122020,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1948,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d399b7ab-efa0-48e3-8602-3102beab604c","uuid":"6ae3b8b9-aa9d-443c-9a5b-01d05c99080e","timestamp":"2026-08-19T20:08:29.810Z"}
